"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"][7]={"school_computer_science_en:M7-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M7-L1 – Propositional logic and digital circuits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Logic, automata and computability<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2 – Conditions and branches<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Represent Boolean expressions\nwith truth tables and gates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When should a fan start?<\/h2>\n<p>A fan should start when a room is too warm or when someone requests\nventilation. If a fault sensor reports a fault, the fan should stay off\nin this model. These three inputs determine the output. To apply the\nrule consistently, we express it as a logical formula, check its truth\ntable and draw the corresponding circuit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Truth values and gates<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>proposition<\/strong> has a truth value in the model: true\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nor false\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nWe use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a heat request,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a manual request and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a fault. A <strong>gate<\/strong> implements a logical operation\nusing digital signals.<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>AND<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∧<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\land<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\noutputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen both inputs are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>OR<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∨<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lor<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\noutputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen at least one input is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThat includes the case where both inputs are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NOT<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>¬<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nreverses a single truth value.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=(H\\lor M)\\land\\neg F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe parentheses make the first sub-decision visible. The circuit\ncalculates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\lor M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin parallel. Both results feed the AND gate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000452_en.svg\"\nalt=\"H and M feed OR. F feeds NOT. Both gates feed AND with output Y. With H=1 and M=0, a fault gives Y=0 and no fault gives Y=1.\" />\n<figcaption>A fault blocks an otherwise active request.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking all inputs to the control rule<\/h2>\n<p>Three independent inputs give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^3=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassignments. List them in binary order and evaluate the sub-expressions\nfirst. In <code>011<\/code>, a manual request is active and a fault has\nbeen reported. Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\lor M=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg F=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\lor M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In Python, <code>and<\/code>, <code>or<\/code> and <code>not<\/code>\nrepresent these operations on Boolean values. The example checks all\neight cases.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>inputs <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(product((<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>), repeat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>outputs <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(hot <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> manual) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> fault <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> hot, manual, fault <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> inputs]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> outputs <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">False<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(value) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> value <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> outputs] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the output for <code>110<\/code>. Use this row to explain why\nexclusive OR would change the model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Express\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncombined with AND and OR. Check the result using <code>101<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add an enable input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhich formula turns the fan on exactly when the original condition holds\nand the enable input is active?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\lor M=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg F=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExclusive OR would give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor two true inputs and leave the output off.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">De Morgan’s laws give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg(A\\land B)=\\neg A\\lor\\neg B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg(A\\lor B)=\\neg A\\land\\neg B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg Y=(\\neg H\\land\\neg M)\\lor F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor <code>101<\/code>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmakes the negation true. The table agrees and gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y&#39;=(H\\lor M)\\land\\neg F\\land E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhenever\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe output is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nregardless of the other inputs.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common errors<\/h2>\n<p>All variables here are truth values available at the same time. A\nsensor needs a threshold rule to turn a measured value into such a\nsignal. The truth table accounts for neither delays nor stored earlier\nstates. Real circuits require separate checks for propagation delay,\nvoltage levels and failure modes. The table alone cannot establish that\na circuit is safe.<\/p>\n<p>When a negation covers several inputs, De Morgan’s laws also change\nthe operators. In a Python expression involving integers,\n<code>&amp;<\/code> and <code>|<\/code> are bitwise operators. The\nspelling with <code>and<\/code>, <code>or<\/code> and <code>not<\/code> is\nclearer for the Boolean inputs used here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A truth table assigns one output to every combination of inputs. The\nstructure of the formula shows how the basic gates are connected. When\ndecisions also depend on earlier inputs, the model needs a state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2 – States and finite\nautomata<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.4 · Represent Boolean\nexpressions with truth tables and gates. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/12/informatik/erhoeht\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 12 – Informatik, erhöhtes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Inf12 LB4 · Represent Boolean expressions with truth tables\nand gates. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M7-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M7-L2 – States and finite automata<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Logic, automata and computability<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L3 – Precise instructions, flowcharts\nand blocks<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design a transition table and a\ndeterministic finite automaton for input strings.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A two-option note<\/h2>\n<p>Imagine reading a long sequence of zeros and ones. At the end, you\nmust decide whether it contains an even number of ones. Counting them\nwould work. Keeping track of whether the count is even or odd is enough.\nA zero leaves the answer unchanged, and a one flips it. Two states\ncapture all the memory that this task requires.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From behaviour to a finite automaton<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>deterministic finite automaton<\/strong> (DFA) has finitely\nmany states and a finite <strong>alphabet<\/strong> of allowed input\nsymbols. A <strong>word<\/strong> is a finite sequence of these symbols.\nThe automaton begins in its <strong>start state<\/strong> and reads the\nword from left to right. Every combination of state and input symbol has\nexactly one next state. If the automaton is in an <strong>accepting\nstate<\/strong> after the last symbol, it accepts the word.<\/p>\n<p>For the alphabet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nan even or odd number of ones read so far.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the start state and the only accepting state. The double circle in\nthe diagram denotes acceptance if the input ends there.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000459_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Start arrow into the double-ringed state G. G and U have loops labelled 0. Each directed link between G and U is labelled 1.\" />\n<figcaption>A one toggles parity while a zero preserves it.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>on <code>0<\/code><\/th>\n<th>on <code>1<\/code><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tracing a word and testing the rule<\/h2>\n<p>For the word <code>10110<\/code> the path is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G \\to U \\to U \\to G \\to U \\to U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe final state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrejects the word. There are indeed three ones. For <code>101<\/code>, the\nsame table leads to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the automaton accepts the word.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>invariant<\/strong> is a statement that holds after\nreading every prefix: the state names the parity of the number of ones\nseen so far, meaning whether it is even or odd. Initially it holds\nbecause zero ones have been read. On reading <code>0<\/code>, neither the\ncount of ones nor the state changes. On reading <code>1<\/code>, both the\nparity and the state change. The invariant therefore holds to the\nend.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>transitions <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;0&quot;<\/span>): <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>, (<span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;1&quot;<\/span>): <span class=\"st\">&quot;O&quot;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    (<span class=\"st\">&quot;O&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;0&quot;<\/span>): <span class=\"st\">&quot;O&quot;<\/span>, (<span class=\"st\">&quot;O&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;1&quot;<\/span>): <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> accepts(word):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    state <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> symbol <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> word:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> symbol <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"st\">&quot;0&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;1&quot;<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Expected a binary word&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        state <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> transitions[state, symbol]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> state <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> accepts(<span class=\"st\">&quot;10110&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> accepts(<span class=\"st\">&quot;101&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> accepts(<span class=\"st\">&quot;&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> accepts(<span class=\"st\">&quot;000&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The code uses <code>E<\/code> for <code>even<\/code> and <code>O<\/code>\nfor <code>odd<\/code>. The states have the same meanings as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Trace <code>0111<\/code>, <code>000<\/code> and the empty word\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhich words are accepted?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Change only the set of accepting states so that exactly the words\nwith an odd number of ones are accepted. Does the automaton still accept\nthe empty word?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A classmate proposes moving from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non <code>0<\/code>. Find a shortest possible counterexample.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>0111<\/code> visits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G,U,G,U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nending in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand is rejected. <code>000<\/code> stays in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is accepted. The empty word triggers no transition.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naccepts it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Make\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe only accepting state. Transitions and the start state stay the same.\nThe empty word still ends in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand is now rejected.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>10<\/code> must be rejected because it contains exactly one\none. The proposed change takes the automaton from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvia\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand accepts it. Words of length zero or one never use the altered\ntransition.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common errors<\/h2>\n<p>An accepting state can have outgoing transitions. Acceptance is\ndecided only at the end of the word. A symbol such as <code>2<\/code>\nfalls outside the alphabet and is an input error in the example.\n<code>0<\/code> is a valid word of length one, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas length zero. A DFA stores only its current state. This limitation\nmatters for tasks that need unbounded memory.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>States summarise the features of the input read so far that matter\nfor the future. The diagram and table describe the same transition\nfunction. The set of accepted words leads to the concept of a formal\nlanguage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3 – Formal languages, patterns\nand grammar<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.3 · Design a transition\ntable and a deterministic finite automaton for input strings. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/13/informatik/grundlegend\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 13 – Informatik, grundlegendes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: Inf13 LB1 · Design a transition table and a\ndeterministic finite automaton for input strings. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M7-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M7-L3 – Formal languages, patterns and grammar<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Logic, automata and computability<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L2 – States and finite automata<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare syntax, semantics,\nregular patterns and a small grammar.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which sequence is allowed?<\/h2>\n<p>A program reads a short command from a device. Before executing the\ncommand, it needs to decide whether the text has the agreed form. “Does\nthe sequence belong to the allowed set?” and “What does this command\nmean?” are separate questions. The first needs a description of the\nstructure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Alphabet, language and grammar<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>alphabet<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsists of the available symbols, for example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{a,b\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains all finite words over that alphabet, including the empty word\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA <strong>formal language<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts <strong>syntax<\/strong> determines which words are allowed.\n<strong>Semantics<\/strong> concerns the meaning assigned to valid words,\nsuch as an action or a numeric value.<\/p>\n<p>The regular pattern <code>a*b<\/code> here stands for zero or more\ncopies of <code>a<\/code> followed by exactly one <code>b<\/code>. A DFA\ncan recognise this pattern. It reads any number of <code>a<\/code>\nsymbols in the start state, enters the accepting state on the first\n<code>b<\/code> and goes to a rejecting sink state on any subsequent\nsymbol. It stays in the sink on either alphabet symbol.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>grammar<\/strong> describes a language using replacement\nrules. It starts from the start symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA <strong>nonterminal<\/strong> such as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be replaced according to a rule. <strong>Terminals<\/strong> such as\n<code>a<\/code> and <code>b<\/code> appear unchanged in the finished word.\nThe rules\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\to aSb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\to\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadd one <code>a<\/code> on the left and one <code>b<\/code> on the right\nas a pair. Derivation trees show which rule creates a branch and where\nreplacement ends.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000435_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The start symbol S branches into a, S and b. The middle S branches again into a, S and b. The innermost S derives the empty word ε.\" />\n<figcaption>Each enclosing rule adds one a and one b.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deriving a word step by step<\/h2>\n<p>The derivation for <code>aabb<\/code> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S \\Rightarrow aSb \\Rightarrow aaSbb \\Rightarrow aabb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first two steps use the first rule. The last step replaces the\nremaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the empty word. Every word generated this way therefore contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncopies of <code>a<\/code> followed by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncopies of <code>b<\/code>, for any non-negative integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConversely, applying the enclosing rule exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes and then the empty rule produces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^n b^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe word <code>abab<\/code> is excluded despite the equal counts. Every\nenclosing replacement places all <code>a<\/code> symbols to the left of\nall <code>b<\/code> symbols.<\/p>\n<p>The grammar generates a <strong>non-regular language<\/strong>. Among\nthe\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprefixes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon,a,\\ldots,a^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na DFA with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstates must take at least two distinct prefixes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nto the same state. After appending\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncopies of <code>b<\/code> to each, it gives the same result for both\nwords. However, it would need to accept\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^i b^i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand reject\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^j b^i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThat is impossible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which of <code>b<\/code>, <code>aab<\/code>, <code>abb<\/code>,\n<code>aa<\/code> and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmatch the pattern <code>a*b<\/code> in full?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive <code>aaabbb<\/code> from the grammar. Then check\n<code>aabbb<\/code> and <code>ba<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do the rules change if at least one pair of <code>a<\/code> and\n<code>b<\/code> must occur?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>b<\/code> matches because zero copies of <code>a<\/code> are\nallowed, and <code>aab<\/code> matches with two copies. <code>abb<\/code>\nhas one <code>b<\/code> too many. <code>aa<\/code> and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlack a final <code>b<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>⇒<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\Rightarrow aSb\\Rightarrow aaSbb\\Rightarrow aaaSbbb\\Rightarrow aaabbb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<code>aabbb<\/code> has different counts of <code>a<\/code> and\n<code>b<\/code>. <code>ba<\/code> puts them in the wrong order. Both are\nexcluded.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Replace the empty rule with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\to ab<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe final replacement always forms the first pair. Further pairs can be\nadded using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\to aSb<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas before.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common errors<\/h2>\n<p>The pattern is applied to the <strong>whole<\/strong> word. Searching\nfor a matching substring would give a different result for\n<code>abb<\/code> because it contains <code>ab<\/code>. In a formal word,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes length zero. The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitself is not entered. Pattern libraries for programming languages can\nprovide extra features beyond the regular languages studied here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Finite automata can recognise regular languages. A grammar specifies\nvalid words through derivation steps and can also generate non-regular\nlanguages. The choice of model depends on which dependencies the words\nmust express.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4 – The Turing model and limits\nof computability<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.3 · Compare syntax,\nsemantics, regular patterns and a small grammar. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/unterricht/faecher-rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplan-informatik-go-teil-c.pdf?ts=1705017673\">Senatsverwaltung\nfür Bildung, Jugend und Familie Berlin – Rahmenlehrplan für die\ngymnasiale Oberstufe – Informatik, Teil C<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2 ·\nCompare syntax, semantics, regular patterns and a small grammar. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M7-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M7-L4 – The Turing model and limits of computability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Logic, automata and computability<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3 – Formal languages, patterns and\ngrammar<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Simulate an abstract machine and\ndistinguish the halting problem from time cost.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What can a computer decide in principle?<\/h2>\n<p>A program has been running for several minutes without a result. The\ninput might require a lot of time, or the program might never stop. Can\nanother program distinguish these cases for <strong>every<\/strong> input\nand program? We use a simple model of executable programs to state that\nquestion precisely.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A tape with changeable contents<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>Turing machine<\/strong> has finitely many control states\nand a tape made of cells. Its head reads and overwrites one cell at a\ntime. A rule for the current state and symbol can specify what to write,\nwhere to move the head and which state comes next. The tape in the model\ncan extend as far as needed. Any finite run still visits only finitely\nmany cells. We use a tape unbounded to the right, start at position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand need only rightward moves in the example. The symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>▫<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\square<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a blank cell.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>halting state<\/strong> ends the run. A\n<strong>configuration<\/strong> records the tape contents, head position\nand control state at one moment. Existing tape contents can be\noverwritten later. This feature distinguishes the model from a DFA that\nreads its input word once.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000464_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Initially q is at position 0 and the tape is 1 blank blank. After one transition q is at position 1, with the tape unchanged. Then h is at position 2, with tape 1 1 blank.\" />\n<figcaption>The second transition overwrites the blank and ends the\ncomputation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Adding one tally mark<\/h2>\n<p>We encode a number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconsecutive ones starting at position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA machine for adding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses a scanning state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a halting state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>State<\/th>\n<th>Read<\/th>\n<th>Write<\/th>\n<th>Move<\/th>\n<th>Next state<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>right<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>▫<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\square<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>right<\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For input <code>1<\/code>, the head starts at position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe machine reads <code>1<\/code>, leaves the cell unchanged and moves\nright to position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The cell at position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis blank. The machine writes <code>1<\/code>, moves to position\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand enters its halting state. The written tape word is <code>11<\/code>,\nwhich encodes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>In general the machine scans past\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nones and writes to one blank cell. The run takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntransitions. That gives a concrete time estimate for this procedure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Trace the same machine on the empty input word and on\n<code>11<\/code>. Give the output word and number of transitions for each\nrun.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Suppose a program\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(p,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalways says “yes” correctly if program\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhalts on input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand “no” otherwise. Define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nso that it runs forever when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(p,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsays “yes” and halts when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(p,p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsays “no”. What would\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(D,D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave to return?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can a time limit of 1000 steps not replace\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom exercise 2?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For empty input, the first transition writes a one and halts. The\nresult is <code>1<\/code> after one transition. On <code>11<\/code>, the\nmachine scans past two ones and writes a third. The result is\n<code>111<\/code> after three transitions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Programs are encoded as input words, so the description of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nitself can be its input. If the answer is “yes”,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nruns forever and the answer is wrong. If the answer is “no”,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(D)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhalts and the answer is also wrong. The assumed general decider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat always halts therefore cannot exist.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A program that halts after step 1001 would still be running after\n1000 steps. The time limit only detects that the run did not end within\nthat window.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common errors<\/h2>\n<p>The contradiction concerns a procedure for <strong>all<\/strong>\nprograms and inputs. Termination can often be proved for specific\nprograms. In the tape example, each scanning step moves the head one\ncell further and the first blank cell ends the scan. Very long running\ntime and undecidability are different properties. The unbounded tape is\na modelling assumption about available storage, separate from the\nproperties of any physical device.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A Turing machine describes a run in terms of transition rules and\nconfigurations. The halting problem places a fundamental limit on\ngeneral automatic decisions. For decidable problems, we can then ask how\nthe cost grows with the input.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5 – Growth rates, P and\nNP<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.3 · Simulate an abstract\nmachine and distinguish the halting problem from time cost. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/unterricht/faecher-rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplan-informatik-go-teil-c.pdf?ts=1705017673\">Senatsverwaltung\nfür Bildung, Jugend und Familie Berlin – Rahmenlehrplan für die\ngymnasiale Oberstufe – Informatik, Teil C<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2 ·\nSimulate an abstract machine and distinguish the halting problem from\ntime cost. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/13/informatik/erhoeht\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 13 – Informatik, erhöhtes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Inf13 LB3–4 · Simulate an abstract machine and distinguish\nthe halting problem from time cost. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/13/informatik/grundlegend\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 13 – Informatik, grundlegendes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: Inf13 LB3 · Simulate an abstract machine and\ndistinguish the halting problem from time cost. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M7-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M7-L5 – Growth rates, P and NP<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Logic, automata and computability<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5 – Trees, graphs and breadth-first\nsearch<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L4 – The Turing model\nand limits of computability<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish practical runtime,\nasymptotic upper bounds and decision problem classes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Does the work grow with the input or with its\npossibilities?<\/h2>\n<p>A planner needs to decide whether someone can visit every place in a\nnetwork of paths exactly once. A proposed order is quick to check.\nTrying all possible orders can take many attempts. These activities,\n<strong>deciding<\/strong> and <strong>checking a proposed\nsolution<\/strong>, lead to different notions of complexity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Cost as a function of input size<\/h2>\n<p>For an algorithm, first choose an input size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an elementary operation, such as a comparison. In linear search,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts the list elements and the worst case compares\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues. Iterating over all subsets of a set of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nelements examines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncandidates. Each element may be included or left out.<\/p>\n<p>The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(n)\\in O(g(n))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes an asymptotic upper bound. There are constants\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(n)\\leq c\\,g(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\geq n_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe bound abstracts away constant factors and small inputs. For example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5n+7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand also in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first is more informative here. An upper bound alone gives neither\nexact seconds nor an automatic matching lower bound.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000437_en.svg\"\nalt=\"At n equal to 4, 8, 16 and 32, n grows linearly. Two to the power of n takes the values 16, 256, 65536 and 4294967296.\" />\n<figcaption>Doubling n affects the two growth models\ndifferently.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision problems and certificates<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>decision problem<\/strong> has a “yes” or “no” answer for\neach valid input. If input size is measured by the length of its agreed\nencoding:<\/p>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>P<\/strong> contains decision problems with a deterministic\ndecision algorithm that runs in polynomial time in the worst case. Its\ncost is bounded above by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor some fixed exponent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><strong>NP<\/strong> contains decision problems whose yes-instances\nhave certificates of polynomially bounded length that a deterministic\nalgorithm can check in polynomial time. No certificate may be accepted\nfor a no-instance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>⊆<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P \\subseteq NP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA decider can dispense with the certificate and decide the input\ndirectly. Whether\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=NP<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an open question.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>In an undirected network, a certificate for a <strong>Hamiltonian\npath<\/strong> lists all places in visit order. Given edges\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{A,B\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{B,C\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{C,D\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand vertices\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B,C,D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<code>A B C D<\/code> passes. The length is correct, each place occurs\nexactly once and each consecutive pair is connected.\n<code>A C B D<\/code> fails at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{A,C\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA checker can build the adjacency matrix once and then look up each\nconsecutive pair. Building the matrix and checking the vertices also\ntake only polynomial time overall. That explains why the decision\nproblem lies in NP. It does not supply an algorithm that decides every\ninstance of the problem in polynomial time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhy are these numbers separate from measured running times?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In a star with centre\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe only edges are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{A,B\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{A,C\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{A,D\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCheck <code>B A C D<\/code> as a certificate. Can any order visit all\nfour places exactly once?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the cost of the “try all orders” procedure fail to prove\nthat the Hamiltonian path problem lies outside P?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The linear model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>16<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>32<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe exponential model gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>65536<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">65536<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>4294967296<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4294967296<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe actual time depends in part on the work per counted operation and on\nthe implementation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The edge\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{C,D\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis missing. Each of the three outer vertices has only one neighbour, so\nit can occur only at an endpoint of a Hamiltonian path. There are only\ntwo endpoints, though. No order passes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The cost describes one particular algorithm. To show that the\n<strong>problem<\/strong> lies outside P, one would have to rule out any\npolynomial-time decider for it.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common errors<\/h2>\n<p>“NP” stands for “nondeterministic polynomial”. Membership in NP does\nnot force a problem to require non-polynomial time. Problems in P are\nalso in NP. A polynomial algorithm can still be impractical when its\nexponent or constant costs are high. Comparing two algorithms fairly\nrequires the same input encoding, counted operations and choice of\ncase.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Growth rates describe the cost of a procedure on large inputs. P and\nNP classify decision problems in terms of efficient decisions and\nefficient checking of yes-certificates, respectively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L6 – Declarative rules and\nqueries<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/13/informatik/erhoeht\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 13 – Informatik, erhöhtes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Inf13 LB4 · Distinguish practical runtime, asymptotic upper\nbounds and decision problem classes. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.2 · Distinguish practical\nruntime, asymptotic upper bounds and decision problem classes. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.claymath.org/millennium/p-vs-np/\">Clay\nMathematics Institute – P vs NP<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Problem\nstatement · Distinguish practical runtime, asymptotic upper bounds and\ndecision problem classes. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M7-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M7-L6 – Declarative rules and queries<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M7 – Logic, automata and computability<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L3 – SQL joins, groups and missing\nvalues<\/a>, <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L3 – Formal languages,\npatterns and grammar<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use facts, rules and queries to\nexpress a declarative model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deriving connections from known facts<\/h2>\n<p>A building directory contains direct connections between named rooms.\nYou want to ask which rooms you can reach from the hall using at least\none connection. In an imperative program, you specify which intermediate\nsteps the computer takes. Here you describe which facts are available\nand under what conditions a further relation holds. An evaluator derives\nthe matching statements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Facts, rules and queries<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>fact<\/strong> describes a known relation, for example\n<code>edge(hall, lab)<\/code>. A <strong>rule<\/strong> has a head and a\nbody. Its head gives the fact that can be derived. Each subgoal in the\nbody must hold for the same assignment of variables. A\n<strong>query<\/strong> asks whether one particular fact can be derived,\nor asks for all assignments to its free variables. In the Datalog-style\nnotation below, uppercase letters denote variables, <code>:-<\/code>\nmeans “holds if”, and a comma in the body joins conditions with AND.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>edge(hall, lab).\nedge(lab, studio).\nedge(studio, office).\n\nreachable(X, Y) :- edge(X, Y).\nreachable(X, Z) :- reachable(X, Y), edge(Y, Z).<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The base rule includes each direct connection. The recursive rule\nextends a previously derived path by one edge. The rules consider\ndirected walks of at least one edge. A room may occur more than once. A\nsimple path, by contrast, would forbid repeated vertices.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/000430_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three solid arrows link hall, lab, studio and office in that order. Dashed arrows lead from the hall to the studio and to the office.\" />\n<figcaption>Repeatedly applying the same rule derives new destinations\nfrom existing facts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deriving facts until the fixed point<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The base rule gives <code>reachable(hall, lab)<\/code>. It also gives\n<code>reachable(lab, studio)<\/code> and\n<code>reachable(studio, office)<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the recursive rule, set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">hall<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\text{hall}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">lab<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=\\text{lab}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">studio<\/mtext><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=\\text{studio}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth conditions in the body hold, so\n<code>reachable(hall, studio)<\/code> follows. This also gives\n<code>reachable(lab, office)<\/code> when applied to the other\nconnection.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">From <code>reachable(hall, studio)<\/code> and\n<code>edge(studio, office)<\/code> we derive\n<code>reachable(hall, office)<\/code>. No new pair appears after this.\nThe set is at the <strong>fixed point<\/strong>. Applying the rules again\nleaves it unchanged.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>The query <code>reachable(hall, Z)<\/code> returns <code>lab<\/code>,\n<code>studio<\/code> and <code>office<\/code>. A <code>JOIN<\/code> query\ncan combine exactly two direct edges:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode sql\"><code class=\"sourceCode sql\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">CREATE<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">TABLE<\/span> edge (src TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>, dst TEXT <span class=\"kw\">NOT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">NULL<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">INSERT<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">INTO<\/span> edge <span class=\"kw\">VALUES<\/span> (<span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;lab&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;lab&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;studio&#39;<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;studio&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&#39;office&#39;<\/span>);<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">SELECT<\/span> e2.dst<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">FROM<\/span> edge <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> e1 <span class=\"kw\">JOIN<\/span> edge <span class=\"kw\">AS<\/span> e2 <span class=\"kw\">ON<\/span> e1.dst <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> e2.src<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">WHERE<\/span> e1.src <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;hall&#39;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-6\"><a href=\"#cb2-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">ORDER<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">BY<\/span> e2.dst;<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>dst<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>studio<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>In this example, the two-edge join gives neither the direct path to\nthe lab nor the three-edge path to the office. A recursive SQL query can\nexpress reachability for arbitrary path lengths. This comparison shows\nthe difference between one fixed join and repeatedly applying the\nrule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does <code>reachable(lab, Z)<\/code> return using the original\nfacts? Justify the results using the rules.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add <code>edge(office, hall)<\/code>. Can\n<code>reachable(hall, hall)<\/code> now be derived? Why can evaluation\nstill finish?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which condition ensures that the two edges in the SQL join form a\ncontinuous path?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>studio<\/code> follows directly from\n<code>edge(lab, studio)<\/code>. <code>office<\/code> follows because the\npath to the studio extends via <code>edge(studio, office)<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes. From the hall, a path goes through the lab, studio and office\nback to the hall. There are only four room names and at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4^2=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistinct ordered pairs. A set stores each pair only once. After finitely\nmany additions, there are no new pairs.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>e1.dst = e2.src<\/code> connects the first edge’s destination\nwith the second edge’s source. <code>e1.src = 'hall'<\/code> additionally\nfixes the starting place.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and common errors<\/h2>\n<p>The fact <code>edge(hall, lab)<\/code> does not imply a reverse edge.\nA relation that cannot be derived from the given facts remains\nunsupported in this model. The real-world data may be incomplete. The\nfixed-point argument assumes finitely many room names, positive rules\nand storage without duplicates. Other rule systems may generate values\nor use different evaluation strategies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Declarative rules specify the conditions for a solution. Base cases\nand a recursive rule generate the set of directed reachability pairs by\nrepeated derivation. The join shown combines exactly two edges.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1 – Sensors, control and\nsimulation<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildung.rlp.de/fileadmin/user_upload/informatik.bildung.rlp.de/Lehrplaene/Informatik_LP_SekII.pdf\">Rheinland-Pfalz,\nLehrplan Informatik für Grund- und Leistungsfach in der Mainzer\nStudienstufe – Lehrplan Informatik – Grund- und Leistungsfach,\ngymnasiale Oberstufe<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.7 · Use facts, rules and\nqueries to express a declarative model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/13/informatik/erhoeht\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 13 – Informatik, erhöhtes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson\nreference: Inf13 LB2 · Use facts, rules and queries to express a\ndeclarative model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.sqlite.org/lang_with.html\">SQLite – The WITH\nClause<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: Recursive Common Table Expressions · Use\nfacts, rules and queries to express a declarative model. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
